> integrate 1/x from 1 to inf numerically qmc
∫ 1/x numerically, x from 1 to inf
  quasi-Monte Carlo (an estimate, not a proof): 10.89064404, standard error 0.64
    8 randomised replicates of 4096 Sobol points in 1 dimension (Joe-Kuo direction numbers, a random digital shift each; seed 1)
    infinite sides onto t in [0, 1), the integrand times the derivative: x = a + t/(1 - t) for [a, inf)
    the error is estimated from the 8 replicates' spread: +-1.5 at 95% if their means are normal (t = 2.365), which nothing proves
    a spike: the largest value met is 2144 times the mean size, so a few points carry the integral; the replicates then agree on a value that is off (usually too small) and their spread understates the error
    not converged: the replicates differ by 6% of the value (standard error); more points, or the integral does not exist (a pole between the points looks like this)
